            <ArticleTitle>[Distal external lymph fistula of the tibia as a complication of vena saphena magna harvesting].</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>85-6</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>In a 71-year-old woman with coronary disease the great saphenous vein was excised for use as an autologous graft vein. Two weeks later a crural ulcer developed at the distal excision site. Conservative treatment by ointment and compression was not successful and plastic surgery was considered. A clear, protein-rich liquid was discharged from the wound. The interdigital injection of methylene blue resulted in appearance of the dye in the ulcer after 45 seconds. Surgery revealed a severed lymph collector. After ligation of the lymph vessel the ulcer within a week.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
